CHAPTER X — COMMAND ECONOMY
The Command Economy is the strategic backbone of Obsidian Protocol.
It determines how many actions a player can take, how far their influence extends, how effectively squads coordinate, and how resilient their forces are under pressure.

This chapter defines:

Command tokens

Command radius

Leadership abilities

Morale

Strategic abilities

Command center loss rules

10.1 Command Tokens
Command tokens represent strategic bandwidth — the player’s ability to issue intents, activate abilities, and coordinate squads.

Token Types
Token Type	Purpose
Tactic Tokens	Issue intents, activate squad abilities
Strategy Tokens	Activate command unit abilities, global actions
Logistics Tokens	Manage supply chains, repair boosts, rerouting
Vector Tokens	Experimental abilities, teleportation, superweapons


Token Generation
Tokens are generated by:

Command Core

Nexus

Insight

Fusion

Vector Core (experimental)

Token Rules
Tokens refresh each cycle

Unused tokens may carry over (limited)

Some abilities require multiple tokens

Token costs scale with tech tree progression

Autonomous Token Use
AI may autonomously:

Spend tactic tokens for emergency retreats

Spend logistics tokens to reroute supply

Request strategy tokens for coordinated assaults

10.2 Command Radius
Command radius defines the area of influence where squads receive bonuses, coordination, and strategic support.

Radius Sources
Command Core

Nexus

Insight

Pulse

Vector Core

Radius Effects
Inside command radius:

Faster intent interpretation

Improved morale

Increased initiative

Faster repairs

Better sensor coverage

Enhanced coordination

Reduced retreat likelihood

Outside command radius:

Slower intent interpretation

Reduced morale

Increased retreat likelihood

Reduced sensor effectiveness

Limited access to advanced abilities

Dynamic Radius
Radius can be:

Expanded by upgrades

Boosted temporarily by abilities

Reduced by EMP

Blocked by hazards

10.3 Leadership Abilities
Leadership abilities are strategic actions provided by command units.

Ability Types
Morale Boosts — increase squad resilience

Initiative Surge — squads act earlier

Sensor Sweep — reveal fog‑of‑war

Pulse Disruption — disable enemy electronics

Fusion Overcharge — boost shields and repairs

Vector Shift — teleport squads

Worldmap Projection — reveal terrain and elevation

Ability Costs
Strategy tokens

Energy

Cooldown time

Command radius presence

Ability AI Behavior
AI may autonomously:

Trigger morale boosts during breaches

Use sensor sweeps before assaults

Deploy pulse disruption against stealth units

Activate fusion overcharge during heavy suppression

10.4 Morale
Morale represents squad confidence and cohesion.

Morale Influencers
Command radius

Leadership abilities

Squad personality

Casualties

Suppression

Elevation advantage

Successful breaches

Environmental hazards

Morale States
State	Effects
High Morale	Increased accuracy, reduced retreat likelihood
Stable Morale	Normal behavior
Shaken	Reduced accuracy, slower movement
Broken	Forced retreat, loss of formation
Routed	Squad flees until regrouped


Morale Recovery
Morale recovers faster:

Inside command radius

Near Command Core

After successful engagements

With support units nearby

10.5 Strategic Abilities
Strategic abilities are high‑impact actions that shape the battlefield.

Examples
Orbital Recon Sweep — reveal entire map section

Command Override — force squads to regroup instantly

Logistics Surge — accelerate supply chains

Pulse EMP — disable enemy command radius

Vector Fold — teleport multiple squads

Worldmap Uplink — increase visibility cones globally

Strategic Ability Rules
Require strategy tokens

Require command unit presence

Have long cooldowns

May require experimental resources

Strategic abilities define late‑game decision‑making.

10.6 Command Center Loss Rules
Losing command infrastructure has severe consequences.

Loss Effects
When Command Core is destroyed:

Command radius collapses

Token generation stops

Morale drops globally

Squad coordination degrades

Autonomous behavior becomes cautious

Production halts

Logistics slow dramatically

Emergency Protocols
Upon Command Core destruction:

Nexus attempts to stabilize command radius

Fusion activates emergency power

Insight boosts sensor coverage

Pulse deploys defensive EMP

Vector Core may teleport key squads to safety

Elimination Rules
A player is eliminated when:

Command Core is destroyed

All command units are destroyed

No logistics nodes remain

No production buildings remain

Command units are the most strategically important assets in the game.
